    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks that makes sense. so if there's a lien or something weird with the paperwork, do they just sit on the money until it's fixed? does the agent have any power to make people settle it or do they just wait around?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, if there's a lien or any issue with the paperwork, the escrow agent will hold onto the funds until the problem is resolved. The escrow agent doesn't have the power to make people settle disputes or fix issues themselves; their role is to facilitate the process and ensure that all parties adhere to the terms of the contract.\n\nIf there's a dispute or a lien, the parties involved (the buyer, the seller, and any other interested parties) will need to work together to resolve the issue. This might involve negotiating with the lienholder, correcting paperwork errors, or taking legal action if necessary. Once the issue is resolved, the escrow agent can release the funds and proceed with the closing process.\n\nIn some cases, the escrow agent may provide a list of unresolved issues to the parties involved, allowing them to address the problems before the closing can proceed. It's important to work with a real estate attorney or professional to navigate any complications that arise during the closing process.",
